entral layout, just for the `jdbc-hikari` module. I used that as ground truth and derived the `jdbc` module's equivalents (module directory names in that repo drop the `micronaut-` prefix, e.g. `jdbc-hikari`, so `micronaut-jdbc` → `jdbc`).\n\n★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────\nCross-checking a sibling metadata entry in the same repo turned out to be more reliable here than a live web fetch would have been: `jdbc-hikari` is a build-time dependency of this very test module (visible in `build.gradle`), so its already-committed URLs are first-party evidence about the parent repo's layout — not a guess. I also grepped every `io.micronaut*` `documentation-url` in the repo to confirm \"Maven Central javadoc jar\" (not the unversioned GitHub Pages guide) is the dominant, already-accepted convention for libraries without a dedicated versioned guide site — this matters because the task explicitly forbids \"latest/current\" docs URLs.\n`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`\n\nHere is the block that needs to replace the current entry in `metadata/io.micronaut.sql/micronaut-jdbc/index.json`:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"latest\" : true,\n  \"metadata-version\" : \"7.1.0\",\n  \"source-code-url\" : \"https://repo1.maven.org/maven2/io/micronaut/sql/micronaut-jdbc/$version$/micronaut-jdbc-$version$-sources.jar\",\n  \"repository-url\" : \"https://github.com/micronaut-projects/micronaut-sql\",\n  \"test-code-url\" : \"https://github.com/micronaut-projects/micronaut-sql/tree/v$version$/jdbc/src/test\",\n  \"documentation-url\" : \"https://repo1.maven.org/maven2/io/micronaut/sql/micronaut-jdbc/$version$/micronaut-jdbc-$version$-javadoc.jar\",\n  \"description\" : \"Micronaut JDBC provides the core Micronaut integration for configuring JDBC javax.sql.DataSource beans and exposing their driver and connection metadata. It supplies the common abstractions and dependency-injection wiring that connection-pool-specific modules, such as Micronaut JDBC Hikari, Tomcat, and DBCP, build on to manage pooled database connections.\",\n  \"tested-versions\" : [\n    \"7.1.0\"\n  ],\n  \"allowed-packages\" : [\n    \"io.micronaut.jdbc\",\n    \"io.micronaut.sql.info\"\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nNo `language` field — micronaut-jdbc is a plain Java library, not language-specific.\n\nTwo things worth flagging before you apply this:\n1. I could not live-verify that `repo1.maven.org/.../micronaut-jdbc/7.1.0/...-sources.jar` and `...-javadoc.jar` actually resolve (HTTP 200), since network access is blocked here — this is inferred from the identical, already-merged pattern for `micronaut-jdbc-hikari` at the same coordinates/groupId.\n2. The test-code-url tag path (`v7.1.0/jdbc/src/test`) assumes the `jdbc` module directory name and monorepo-wide version tagging, mirrored from the `jdbc-hikari` sibling — also not independently re-verified live.\n\nWould you like me to proceed once you can grant write/network permissions, or would you rather apply this JSON yourself?\nRan agent for io.micronaut.sql:micronaut-jdbc:7.1.0 (exit 0). Log: /home/mihailo/git/graalvm-reachability-metadata/forge/local_repositories/forge_worktrees/9808-892f5449/build/agent-url-discovery-logs/io.micronaut.sql-micronaut-jdbc-7.1.0.log\nCompleted populateArtifactURLs: invoked=1, prompted=0, skipped=0, overwriteExisting=false, verifyArtifactSources=false, limit=null\n\n[Incubating] Problems report is available at: file:///home/mihailo/git/graalvm-reachability-metadata/forge/local_repositories/forge_worktrees/9808-892f5449/build/reports/problems/problems-report.html\n\nDeprecated Gradle features were used in this build, making it incompatible with Gradle 10.\n\nYou can use '--warning-mode all' to show the individual deprecation warnings and determine if they come from your own scripts or plugins.\n\nFor more on this, please refer to https://docs.gradle.org/9.1.0/userguide/command_line_interface.html#sec:command_line_warnings in the Gradle documentation.\n\nBUILD SUCCESSFUL in 2m 30s\n9 actionable tasks: 1 executed, 8 up-to-date\n"}], "isError": false, "timestamp": 1790905905324}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_4KqNjHsqWRQCsJMOHgGhLWjF|fc_070a861bb27c5259016abf0e31122487d1894dfba621646443", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Based on my research, here's the decision:\n\n**Key finding:** `io.micronaut.sql:micronaut-jdbc` is the *shared abstraction* module in Micronaut SQL — it supplies `io.micronaut.jdbc.*` classes (`DataSourceResolver`, `BasicJdbcConfiguration`, `CalculatedSettings`, the `io.micronaut.jdbc.metadata.*` pool-metadata abstractions) but registers no concrete `javax.sql.DataSource` bean itself. Concrete pools live in sibling artifacts (`micronaut-jdbc-hikari`, `-tomcat`, `-dbcp`, `-ucp`), which this repo already supports — `metadata/io.micronaut.sql/micronaut-jdbc-hikari` and its test at `tests/src/io.micronaut.sql/micronaut-jdbc-hikari/7.1.2` prove the normal-usage pattern: a pool implementation + `com.h2database:h2` as an in-memory driver, wired through `ApplicationContext`, no Docker or env vars needed.\n\nWithout a pool implementation on the classpath, a scaffold depending on `micronaut-jdbc` alone can't produce a live `DataSource` bean, so the DI-driven paths through the core package (`DataSourceResolver` unwrapping, pool metadata providers) would go untested — only static utility classes would be reachable. This is the same shape as a precedent I found in `stats/org.typelevel/cats-effect-std_3/3.5.2/execution-metrics.json`: a \"standard library\" module that needs its runtime counterpart declared explicitly since it isn't a transitive dependency.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"action\": \"advisory_preparation\",\n  \"summary\": \"micronaut-jdbc is the shared core/abstraction module (io.micronaut.jdbc.*: DataSourceResolver, BasicJdbcConfiguration, CalculatedSettings, pool-metadata abstractions). It registers no concrete javax.sql.DataSource bean itself — real usage always pairs it with a pool implementation (hikari/tomcat/dbcp/ucp), which are separate, non-transitive artifacts.\",\n  \"deterministic_setup\": [\n    {\n      \"kind\": \"dependency\",\n      \"coordinate\": \"io.micronaut.sql:micronaut-jdbc-hikari:7.1.0\",\n      \"scope\": \"testImplementation\",\n      \"reason\": \"Provides the concrete HikariCP-backed DataSource and DatasourceConfiguration beans needed to drive the core jdbc module's DataSourceResolver and pool-metadata classes through a live ApplicationContext; this repo already tests micronaut-jdbc-hikari (metadata/io.micronaut.sql/micronaut-jdbc-hikari) using the same pattern, confirming it is not pulled in transitively by micronaut-jdbc.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"kind\": \"dependency\",\n      \"coordinate\": \"com.h2database:h2:2.1.210\",\n      \"scope\": \"testImplementation\",\n      \"reason\": \"In-memory JDBC driver needed to actually open pooled connections and exercise the core module's driver-detection and metadata code paths; same version already used by the sibling micronaut-jdbc-hikari test.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"agent_guidance\": \"Scope reachability metadata and the user-code filter to the io.micronaut.jdbc.* core package (not io.micronaut.configuration.jdbc.hikari, which belongs to the sibling artifact). Configure a datasource via ApplicationContext.builder().properties(...) (datasources.default.url/driver-class-name/username/password), then fetch javax.sql.DataSource and io.micronaut.jdbc.metadata.DataSourcePoolMetadataProvider/DataSourcePoolMetadata beans from the context to exercise the core abstractions directly, in addition to any standalone unit tests on classes that don't need a container.\",\n  \"risks\": [\n    \"Micronaut SQL submodules are normally released in lockstep from one multi-module repo, so micronaut-jdbc-hikari:7.1.0 should exist alongside micronaut-jdbc:7.1.0, but this wasn't independently verified against Maven Central in this pass — local Gradle resolution is authoritative.\",\n    \"If tests only call static/standalone utility classes without a live ApplicationContext, coverage of the DI-driven classes in io.micronaut.jdbc.* will be shallow even with the pool dependency present.\"\n  ]\n}\n```\n"}], "isError": false, "timestamp": 1790905905324}]}
